{
"dur": 42.722,
"shots": [
{
"i": 1,
"start": 0.0,
"vo_start": 0.6,
"vo_end": 8.24,
"text": "Russia has one of the world's biggest submarine fleets. Many patrol the North Atlantic, near the gap between Greenland, Iceland and Britain.",
"end": 8.515
},
{
"i": 2,
"start": 8.515,
"vo_start": 8.79,
"vo_end": 15.47,
"text": "Some subs can cut undersea data cables. Others hunt ships. And sea mines can close a whole shipping lane.",
"end": 15.745
},
{
"i": 3,
"start": 15.745,
"vo_start": 16.02,
"vo_end": 23.433,
"text": "Our ship makes it to the North Sea, nearly at Rotterdam. Then the port's computer systems go dark. A cyber attack.",
"end": 23.708
},
{
"i": 4,
"start": 23.708,
"vo_start": 23.983,
"vo_end": 33.702,
"text": "The captain can't wait. He turns for Bremerhaven in Germany instead. US forces have practised using ports all over Europe for exactly this reason.",
"end": 33.977
},
{
"i": 5,
"start": 33.977,
"vo_start": 34.252,
"vo_end": 41.772,
"text": "Meanwhile soldiers from Fort Stewart, Georgia, board ordinary airliners. The US military can borrow commercial jets in a crisis.",
"end": 42.722
}
],
